cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it using the metadata below."
title: "Donghyunre (동현레): a browser laboratory for one-on-one skirmish battles on the Command & Conquer: Red Alert engine (Vanilla Conquer built to WebAssembly), with the two sides, starting credits, tech level, computer skill, map and starting units set through the esoteric language Donghyun-lang"
type: software
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.24430548"
version: "0.12"
date-released: "2026-11-22"
authors:
  - family-names: Kim
    given-names: Dong Hyun
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9883-0229"
    affiliation: "Inha University College of Medicine"
license: GPL-3.0-or-later
repository-code: "https://github.com/Raspberry-Kim/donghyun-lab19"
url: "https://raspberry-kim.github.io/donghyun-lab19/"
abstract: >-
  EA has not endorsed and does not support this product.
  Donghyunre is a browser-based, noncommercial toy in which the player
  builds a base, harvests ore and fights one computer opponent in a
  one-on-one skirmish. It runs a modified copy of Vanilla Conquer (commit
  9103a8e), a portable version of the Command & Conquer: Red Alert source
  code that Electronic Arts released under the GNU General Public License
  version 3 with additional terms, compiled to WebAssembly with
  Emscripten 3.1.1. A program written in the esoteric programming
  language Donghyun-lang prints seven integers that are read in order as
  the player's side, the opponent's side, starting credits (500 to
  10000), tech level (1 to 10), computer skill (1 to 3), map (1 to 6) and
  number of starting units (0 to 12). All five sides of the original
  (Russia, England, Ukraine, Germany, France) can be chosen for either
  player, each with its one multiplier from the original rules. Six
  symmetric maps were newly made for this work. The engine was changed to
  start a battle directly from these values, to let the computer's side be
  chosen, to save and resume a skirmish in the browser, to scroll the map
  with the arrow keys and to report victory and defeat to the page. There
  is no campaign, no music, no video and no multiplayer; the text and
  speech inside the game are in English. Version 0.12 adds save and load buttons on the page (three browser
  slots and a downloadable .dhsave file that carries the engine's own
  save file), pauses the battle while the save menu is open, and makes
  the arrow keys scroll the map also when keyboard focus is on the page
  or after a drag is released outside the game screen; rules and maps are
  unchanged.
  The original game data (graphics, sound effects and speech of the
  freeware release, without music and video) is not part of this
  repository or of this archive. It remains the property of Electronic
  Arts Inc., is not under the GPL, and is fetched at run time from a
  separate data-only repository under EA's modding guidelines
  (noncommercial, revocable). The whole repository, including the
  modified engine source in engine/Vanilla-Conquer and the patch
  engine/donghyun-re.patch, is distributed under the GNU General Public
  License version 3 with the additional terms of Electronic Arts Inc.
  reproduced in COPYING; the page fonts are under the SIL Open Font
  License. Command & Conquer and Red Alert are trademarks of Electronic
  Arts Inc.; this work is unaffiliated. Tests are automated browser runs;
  no full game was played to the end by a person and no real device was
  checked.
keywords:
  - real-time strategy
  - Command & Conquer Red Alert
  - Vanilla Conquer
  - WebAssembly
  - esoteric programming language
